d the groundwork for  a decade of high-energy action sequences  burtweed played the doctor as a scrapper  and a scientist making him equally quick  with a gadget as a fist the third doctor  was angry dude being forced to hang  around earth and he took out his anger  on any alien who got in the way of his  fists his tenure is an important  building block in several other ways  marking the first appearance of sarah  jane smith as a companion introducing  the doctor's nemesis the master and  reviving the daleks as primary  antagonists  still his tenure is firmly in the middle  of the pack reversed neutron flow or no  peter capaldi was previously best known  for his work as a foul-mouthed political  operative and fittingly he brought a  good bit of darkness back to the role in  his brief run as the doctor he played  the character with a cold remove amping  up the alienness of the time lord in a  way that hadn't been seen since the  original run of the show far from david  tennant's near romance with a human  capaldi's doctor was a weary ancient  outsider who showed up to help but had  almost no bedside manner to speak of  showrunner stephen moffat noted the new  iteration's coldness and he called  capaldi's doctor much fiercer madder and  less reliable than tenant or matt smith  the characters around the doctor  recognize his weakness as his run goes  on having to balance their plan of  attack against antagonists while knowing  that 12 is an adrenaline junkie who  loves to dive in with both feet  live by the moffat die by the moffett  matt smith's eccentric and noodly  performance as the doctor was  characterized by a head writer who loved  rousing speeches front loading his run  with memorable standoffs and scenery  chewing shouting matches however smith  was also in the main role when stephen  moffat spiraled the series into a  convoluted mess that couldn't just be  waved away with wiggly wobbly tiny  whimey  stuff for many of the episodes smith is  a perfect first doctor for the show's  intended audience  children his giddy joy over dinosaurs on  a spaceship and his wonder at seeing the  perseverance of people well into the  future makes him an ideal hero for a  child  i know  dinosaurs  on a spaceship  however much of that goodwill is  squandered by convoluted plotlines that  can be difficult to understand even as  an adult to follow the story of the  doctor amy and rory late in matt smith's  tenure was a good way to risk a  nosebleed but the highs of his first  appearance and a chills inducing stare  down between the doctor and every major  villain were worth the headache  if patrick trouten only managed to get  people on board with the idea of an  entirely new actor playing the doctor  and in only 150 episodes no less his  work would be admirable the fact that  treven went above and beyond that  securing a place above the original  actor in most fans hearts is remarkable  credit a shift in writing and costuming  to play detroit's strengths the  so-called cosmic hobo turned the doctor  from a lecturer to a lovable  space-faring [ __ ] troutman played the  doctor with the wide-eyed wonder you  might expect from someone who has the  ability to travel the entire universe  the hobo aesthetic leeched into the  script trodden's doctor got in and out  of stories much quicker than hartnell's  four-part historical epics cementing the  monster of the week style story that has  produced some of doctor who's most  thrilling hours trodden helped build the  antagonism between classic villains and  the doctor over his three series tenure  as he nearly always encountered a  villain every time he stepped out of the  police box  christopher eccleston gave us some idea  of how a longer run for john hurt's war  doctor would play out the first doctor  of the 2005 doctor who revival played  the character with all the weight of the  time war on his shoulders his outfit  reflected his brooding nature suiting  the alien traveler up in a leather  jacket and topping it off with an angry  stare his take on the doctor preceded  the grimdark era of genre movies but his  performance can be seen as an early  adopter of that gritty style in  particular he earns his dour attitude in  the modern classic episode the doctor  dances his harsh demeanor cracks when he  learns that his crew has managed to set  things right without causing death and  destruction in an instantly iconic  moment eccleston turns to the camera  with a maniacal grin on his face and  shouts  everybody lives bros  just this once  everybody lives  his tenure on who brought the show back  to life and that one crack allowed light  to shine into the series as they  barreled toward one of the series most  adored leads  for many people born in the late 80s on  david tennant is the doctor his affable  but confident turn as the doctor brought  a massive wave of new fans who certainly  weren't turned away by the fact that the  writing hit a high for knew who during  tenants time balancing the steady hand  of russell t davies with the bursts of  originality from stephen moffat tennant  was also buoyed by a murderer's row of  companions in billy piper's lovesick  rose catherine tate's wisecracking donna  and frima adjamen's intelligent and  assured martha tennant's run also  introduced the tragic plot of river song  and included several all-time great  episodes like blink and human nature  altogether the writing and cast paired  with a budget reflecting their suddenly  high ratings produced the best run of  the series since its revival  don't turn your back don't look away and  don't blink  tom baker what can we say if you're over  a certain age his woolly hair and long  scarf are bound to be the image in your  mind's eye when you recall the doctor  he's lodged there for more reasons than  the fact that he held the position  longer than any other actor though that  certainly doesn't hurt his case for best  doctor ever tom baker hit the perfect  balance between a 